{"name":"Big Stone","hex":"#334046","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Big Stone","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A long, narrow freshwater lake and reservoir on the border between western Minnesota and northeastern South Dakota in the United States."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Big_Stone"}],"entries":[{"title":"Big Stone Lake","blocks":[{"text":"Big Stone Lake is a long, narrow freshwater lake and reservoir on the border between western Minnesota and northeastern South Dakota in the United States."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Big_Stone_Lake_from_Ortonville.JPG/330px-Big_Stone_Lake_from_Ortonville.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Big_Stone_Lake","wikidata":"Q859342"},{"title":"Rock (geology)","blocks":[{"text":"In geology, a rock is any naturally occurring solid mass or aggregate of minerals or mineraloid matter. It is categorized by the minerals included, its chemical composition, and the way in which it is formed. Rocks form the Earth's outer solid layer, the crust, and most of its interior, except for the liquid outer core and pockets of magma in the asthenosphere. The study of rocks involves multiple subdisciplines of geology, including petrology and mineralogy. It may be limited to rocks found on Earth, or it may include planetary geology that studies the rocks of other celestial objects."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Marsh_Butte_and_Geikie_Peak%2C_Grand_Canyon.jpg/330px-Marsh_Butte_and_Geikie_Peak%2C_Grand_Canyon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rock_(geology)","wikidata":"Q8063"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Kummakivi_balancing_rock_in_Ruokolahti%2C_Finland.jpg/500px-Kummakivi_balancing_rock_in_Ruokolahti%2C_Finland.jp